{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Words of.</b> It is written: \"And he spoke three thousand proverbs, and his song was…\" etc. (1 Kings 5:12). And the mashal is that one thing is compared in it, like posing a riddle; and mashal — he uttered a parable, and it is the great eagle, and what is compared is Nebuchadnezzar. And the way of song is to be about something past or something yet to be. And because Solomon's desire was to compose words that would arise upon the heart, he began with the word \"words of,\" and it is not like \"all my words\" (all that I said), for the verse that follows it indicates it, or it itself also indicates [this]; the second verse in this book [indicates] all the first. And Qohelet is Solomon, for there was no anointed king from the sons of David except him alone, and he is called thus on account of the wisdom that was assembled (נקהלה) in him. And he mentioned the name of the place of his kingdom on account of its glory — do you not see that he is called king of Jerusalem, king of Salem (Malki-Tzedek), for it is Salem, and it indicates him: \"and in Salem is his booth\" (Psalm 76:3). And likewise Adoni-Tzedek, on account of his being lord of the place of justice; and likewise Devir, king of Salem (Malki-Tzedek), and he is King David."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The phrase ודרך השיר להיות תחלה או דבר עתיד להיו׳ is slightly truncated in the source (עתיד להיו׳ = להיות); rendered literally. The final identification of Malki-Tzedek as King David follows the reading דוד המלך; some witnesses may vary."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:27:09.321435001Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}